--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
WHAT THE WEB PAGE IS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
ActiveSessionsPage.tsx is a thin promotion wrapper: it sets a PageContainer
(title, subtitle, copyLink) and embeds <ActiveSessionsSection /> verbatim, so the
active browser/device-session list (per-row "Sign out" + a footer "Sign out all
other devices", both step-up-gated upstream by RequireSudo) lives at
/account/sessions with a real page title instead of being a <section id="security">
block inside the Settings page. The page itself declares no data of its own (the
parity manifest records zero dataSources, zero panels, and exactly two strings —
requiredCount=2: account.sessions.title + account.sessions.subtitle); the embedded
ActiveSessionsSection owns the list query (useSessions — the open-mode vs
forward-auth union), the two revoke mutations (useRevokeSession /
useRevokeAllOtherSessions), the per-row user-agent->device heuristic, both confirm
dialogs, and the empty / loading / error branches.

--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
NATIVE IMPLEMENTATION (no placeholders, ADR-011; no delegation)
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
The shared A3 ActiveSessionsSection feature-view already exists
(io.teslasync.android.featureviews.activesessionssection.ActiveSessionsSection)
and owns every session data state for its cache-then-network feed: the loading
spinner inside the panel chrome, the AUTH_MODE_OPEN amber advisory, the
forward-auth DataTable (device / IP / signed-in / last-seen) with the "This device"
badge, the per-row "Sign out" ghost button + the footer "Sign out all other
devices" button, both no-silence ConfirmDialogs, the DataTable empty branch, the
hard-error + retry surface, and the freshness/stale-offline auto-refresh. This page
is its host: it sets the PageContainer-equivalent header and embeds the feature
view — never re-implementing it (DRY, ADR-006). This mirrors the sibling
notifications/ChannelsPage (a 2-string wrapper that embeds NotificationChannelsView)
and notifications/ArchivedPage (embeds InboxBody).

The page binds the feed to the cross-platform P1/S8 shared state holder
io.teslasync.shared.core.presentation.sessions.SessionsStore (the KMP port of the
web useSessions domain) over the shared S7 HttpSessionRepository, so the 30s
staleTime, the 501 AUTH_MODE_OPEN open-mode normalisation, and the
invalidate-on-revoke rule come from the shared layer unchanged (ADR-004) — no HTTP
is re-implemented in the Android module.

New files (package io.teslasync.android.settings.sessions):
